<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Shirley</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1694">1694</a>–<a href="/years/1771">1771</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Shirley was a British lawyer and governor of the Province of Massachusetts Bay from 1741 to 1749 and 1753 to 1756. He organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> expedition that captured the French fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisbourg">Louisbourg</a> in 1745 and briefly commanded British forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-and-indian-war">French and Indian War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-shirley.jpg" alt="William Shirley" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Hudson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WilliamShirleyNPG.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William Shirley was born on 2 December 1694 at Preston Manor in East Sussex, studied at Pembroke College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, and read law at the Inner Temple. An inheritance of about £10,000 from his grandfather in 1717 did not survive his expensive way of life and the depression of 1721, and with eight children by 1731 he sought a post in the colonies. He arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> that year with letters from the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle</a>, a relation by marriage and his patron, and became advocate general in the admiralty court. Allied with the merchant Samuel Waldo, he worked to unseat Governor Jonathan Belcher and replaced him in August 1741.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Shirley began by settling a currency crisis left by the dissolved land bank. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> declared war in March 1744, he helped organise the expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisbourg">Louisbourg</a>, which surrendered on 17 June after a siege that cost the provincials 180 men. Plans he then pressed for the conquest of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> came to nothing when support from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> failed. Disputes over the war's costs took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1749, and he joined the commission set up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> and France to fix colonial borders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, whose failure his hard line helped bring about.</p><p>Back in Massachusetts from 1753, he sent an expedition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kennebec-river">Kennebec River</a> that built Fort Halifax, led a force to reinforce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-oswego">Fort Oswego</a> in 1755 and became Commander-in-Chief, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, after the death of General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-braddock">Edward Braddock</a>. His feud with the Indian agent William Johnson and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> politicians, and suspicion over intercepted letters, led to his recall as both commander-in-chief and governor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Shirley later served as governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a>, then returned to Massachusetts, where he died on 24 March 1771. His son William was killed in 1755 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-monongahela">Battle of the Monongahela</a>; his elder son Thomas became a major general and a baronet. The house Shirley built at Roxbury between 1747 and 1751, now the Shirley-Eustis House, is still a museum, and the town of Shirley, Massachusetts, and Shirley Point in Winthrop are named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William Shirley” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Massachusetts led New England's wars against New France, and in 1745 forces raised by governor <b>William Shirley</b> captured Louisbourg on Ile Royale.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-massachusetts-bay">Province of Massachusetts Bay</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Preston, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Roxbury, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.76,-2.7&amp;year=1744" aria-label="Preston on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of William Shirley"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4864 5530h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4864 5530h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.76,-2.7&amp;year=1744">What was here in 1744? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
